    {
      "headline": "Scientists Construct Atom-Sized Pores That Mimic Living Ion Channels for Advanced Molecular Control",
      "description": "Researchers at the University of Osaka engineered a chemistry-driven autonomous nanopore membrane that forms and controls subnanometer-scale pores — approximately the size of single atoms — by exploiting electrochemically triggered precipitation inside a silicon nitride nanopore. When a negative voltage is applied, a precipitate forms and narrows the pore; reversing the voltage dissolves it, reopening the passage. This voltage-switchable system closely mimics the gating behavior of biological ion channels. The study was published in Nature Communications.",
      "context": "Biological ion channels are among the most precise molecular machines in nature, controlling nerve impulses, muscle contractions, and cellular signaling through angstrom-scale gating. Fabricating artificial analogs at this scale has been a persistent challenge in nanotechnology. The Osaka team's approach — using electrochemistry to autonomously form and erase ultra-small pores — opens pathways to single-molecule DNA sequencing, neuromorphic computing architectures that mimic neural spiking behavior, nanoreactors for confined chemical synthesis, and next-generation ion-selective membranes for energy and water applications.",
      "timeline": [
        {"type": "background", "date": "2020-01-01", "label": "Decade-long effort to fabricate subnanometer synthetic pores matching biological ion channel dimensions hits fundamental diffraction and fabrication precision limits."},
        {"type": "escalation", "date": "2025-06-01", "label": "University of Osaka team develops miniature electrochemical reactor concept for in-situ pore formation inside silicon nitride nanopores."},
        {"type": "event", "date": "2026-02-18", "label": "Study published in Nature Communications: voltage-controlled subnanometer pores demonstrated with autonomous open/close gating behavior mimicking biological ion channels."},
        {"type": "consequence", "date": "2026-03-18", "label": "Research widely cited; applications in DNA sequencing, neuromorphic computing, and nanoreactor design identified as near-term development pathways."}
      ],
      "importance_score": 4,
      "category": "science",
      "region": "Asia",
      "key_terms": [
        {"term": "Nanopore", "definition": "An extremely small pore in a membrane, often at the nanometer or angstrom scale, used for molecular sensing, sequencing, or ion transport applications."},
        {"term": "Ion Channel", "definition": "Protein structures in biological cell membranes that regulate ion flow through angstrom-scale gating, generating electrical signals essential for nerve and muscle function."},
        {"term": "Electrochemical Reactor", "definition": "The nanoscale reaction chamber created inside the silicon nitride nanopore, where voltage-driven precipitation forms and dissolves the subnanometer pore."},
