      "headline": "New Research Identifies Neural Mechanism in the Brain Responsible for Breaking Old Habits",
      "description": "Researchers at the Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology (OIST) used two-photon microscopy to observe real-time acetylcholine release by cholinergic interneurons in the striatum of mice navigating a virtual maze. When the expected reward path was changed without warning, the mice experienced loss of an expected reward — and the team observed a significant surge of acetylcholine in specific striatal regions. This spatially heterogeneous acetylcholine signal was identified as the key trigger enabling behavioral flexibility and the breaking of established habits. The study was published in Nature Communications.",
      "context": "Understanding how the brain breaks habits has major implications for treating addiction, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), and Parkinson's disease — all conditions where habit or behavior flexibility is pathologically disrupted. Acetylcholine levels are already altered by many neuropsychiatric treatments, but the precise spatial and temporal patterns of its release during habit disruption were previously unmeasured. This study provides the first real-time molecular-level evidence of the striatal cholinergic mechanism governing behavioral flexibility, opening pathways to more targeted pharmacological and neurostimulation therapies.",
      "timeline": [
