    {
      "headline": "European Medicines Agency Launches New PRIME Tools to Accelerate Development of Critical Medicines",
      "description": "The European Medicines Agency (EMA) formally launched three new permanent tools within its PRIME (PRIority MEdicines) scheme on March 18, 2026, following a successful pilot period. The three tools are: a regulatory roadmap and product development tracker that charts a medicine's progress and flags issues early; expedited scientific advice providing fast-track regulatory input on critical development questions; and a submission readiness meeting held approximately one year before application submission to identify evidence gaps. The EMA will integrate all three as permanent PRIME features ahead of the revised EU pharmaceutical legislative framework.",
      "context": "PRIME, launched in 2016, has supported the accelerated development of medicines targeting unmet medical needs — areas where patients currently have no treatment options or where existing therapies offer insufficient benefit. The three new tools address the scheme's identified weaknesses by institutionalizing continuous dialogue, flexible advice, and pre-submission alignment. Their codification in the upcoming revised EU pharmaceutical legislation ensures long-term regulatory certainty for drug developers and could meaningfully shorten the timeline from drug discovery to patient access for priority therapies.",
      "timeline": [
